# worktree-spinoff
## Repository tracker (optional)


Use issuectl only when **both** repository instructions or an issuectl schema
establish that this repository uses it **and** its CLI is available. A directory named `issues/` alone may belong to another tracker;
check the repository policy before treating it as issuectl storage. This probe
checks the conventional schema-backed case from the repository root:

```sh
# issuectl-capability-probe
if [ ! -f issues/.schema.yaml ]; then
  printf 'tracker-not-used\n'
elif command -v issuectl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  printf 'issuectl-ready\n'
else
  printf 'issuectl-cli-missing\n'
fi
```

For a documented issuectl tree without that schema path, verify configuration
by reading repository instructions or tracker files, not by running issuectl
against an unidentified `issues/` tree. Check CLI availability separately.
Do not install or initialize a tracker. Without both capabilities, use the free-text task and committed
repository artifacts; skip issue lookup, mutation, closure, trailers, and DAG
commands. If an explicit issue-driven request requires unavailable tracker data
or a required mutation, report that prerequisite and stop before spawning;
do not silently reinterpret a slug or hand-edit tracker metadata. A CLI command
failure in an enabled tracker is an error, not permission to switch modes.
`issuectl-cli-missing` is a disclosed missing capability, not proof that the
repository is tracker-free. If repository policy requires tracker scheduling or
writes for this task/stint, stop; only policy-permitted free-text work may use
the no-issuectl path. Record follow-up findings in the native report's
`spinoff_proposals` or `discussion_items` instead of creating tracker files.
State this capability result as a fact in every worker brief; it does not relax
the generated policy on how an issue, if filed, must be created.


# taskfleet-spawn-spinoff
## Parent wait and delivery


Use a plain blocking process:

```bash
taskfleet run wait <run-id> --output json
```

Start it through the parent harness's existing background-command/wake facility
when one exists, requesting a wake on exit, then continue independent work or end
the turn. No notification hook, desktop, or attached terminal is required. Do not
shell-background it, invent a callback transport, or poll `run show` for wakeups.
Without a wake facility, await the same blocking command with `--timeout`
shorter than the harness's foreground-command limit. On `timed-out`, establish
another bounded wait if continued waiting is appropriate; do not treat the
timeout as completion. If the harness kills the process without an envelope,
inspect `run show` once and re-establish the wait. Never promise an automatic
later message or interpret a killed observer as a failed worker. After a parent restart, inspect the
known run and establish a fresh wait if needed, rather than create a duplicate.

Read `data.outcome` and `data.runs[]`: timeout is not completion, and attention,
stall, or awaiting-input can settle a wait without a terminal success. Resolve
that condition; `--terminal` waits for the eventual terminal result but answers
no decision. Then read `taskfleet run show <run-id> --output json` for the full
`data.report`, `landed`, `landed_method`, and `preserved_work`. A settled run,
clean worker exit, visible UI entry, or disappearing window is not merge proof.
Taskfleet's CLI and supervisor retain merge, reporting, and teardown authority.


# worktree
## Runtime capabilities


Read sibling instructions through this harness's skill loader or file-reading
facility; no particular invocation tool is required. Bundled text is also
available with `taskfleet skill print <name>`. External review, Git-helper,
and session-saving skills mentioned here are optional unless the user or
repository mandates them. If absent, use ordinary Git, evidence-based self-review,
and repository-local handoff notes instead. A missing **required** review or
capability blocks completion and is disclosed, never claimed as run. Do not
install tools, change another user's configuration, or publish artifacts merely
to satisfy a skill reference.


# worktree
## Handing over


Announce the choice in one line before invoking anything, for example
`Routing to /worktree-research — survey-of-options framing on an inquiry topic.`
That line is the user's chance to interrupt before a worktree exists.

Then invoke the sibling through the harness's skill loader or by reading its instructions with `$ARGUMENTS` unchanged:
the task text, any flags, in the order the user wrote them. Flags such as
`--headless`, `--tmux-session`, `--profile`, `--interactive`, or a leading
`--review` mean something specific to the sibling that receives them, and some
are not `taskfleet` flags at all but instructions the sibling folds into the
brief. Stripping, normalizing, or rephrasing here changes the brief the worker
will eventually receive, and rejecting an unknown flag is the sibling's job, with
the sibling's error message. Likewise do not expand or "helpfully" enrich the
task; the sibling owns the brief and knows what the worker needs.

If the selected bundled sibling is not loaded, read it with
`taskfleet skill print <name>` and execute those instructions. If neither a
loader nor readable bundled text is available, disclose the missing prerequisite
and stop before creating state. Preserve the original arguments unchanged.

Route to one sibling, once. If loading or executing the sibling reports a failure, tell the user
what it said rather than trying another sibling: the failed attempt may already
have created a run or branch, and a second attempt for the same task can leave
two of them. Do not chain siblings ("research first, then fan out"); the user
invokes the second step when the first has landed.


# worktree-bug-analysis
## Without issuectl: analyse an existing report artifact


When the optional repository tracker is not enabled, accept an existing
repository bug-report path or an explicit free-text report, not an unresolved
issuectl slug. Agree an exact output path (default `research/<slug>-bug-analysis.md`)
where `<slug>` is a short kebab-case title derived from the report, not an
issue id; use it in the run title too. Copy the report and relevant attachments
into the brief as paths/content.
Do not file an issue just to run an analysis. If the request supplies only an
unavailable issue reference, report the missing source and stop before spawning.

Use the native spinoff creation, closing recipe, and full failure-disclosure
sections below, but skip the issue-specific lookup, status, trailer, and storage
instructions. The self-contained brief asks for the verdict, severity, affected
area, reproduction attempts, examined revision, and fix sketch under the exact
heading `## Triage analysis` in the agreed report file. Commit only that report
and supporting analysis artifacts, never application changes or tracker
metadata. The user retains disposition authority. After the plain parent wait,
read the native report and verify the analysis landed at the agreed path.


# stint-start
## Without issuectl: a bounded serial round


If the optional repository tracker is not enabled, use this path instead of
all tracker-specific round instructions below. Read repository instructions,
the current handoff if present, and the user's accepted task. Confirm a clean,
committed source branch; synchronize only where repository remote policy calls
for it. No upstream or deployment requirement is invented for a local-only repo.
If there is no accepted scope, agree one with the user before spawning.

Choose one focused free-text task with explicit done criteria and the exact
repository validation commands. Inspect `taskfleet run list --repo . --output
json` and known run reports for overlapping live or retained work; if ownership
or overlap cannot be established, resolve that before spawning. Do not adopt or
cancel another session's runs. Do not create a private DAG, lane table, issue
backlog, or reservation file. Sequence tasks: spawn only the next accepted task
after its predecessor has landed. Use `worktree-spinoff` (or the bundled
`taskfleet-spawn-spinoff` primitive) and a self-contained brief including the
closing recipe, failure disclosure, and proportionate review. If a required
reviewer is unavailable, report that blocker rather than weaken the brief.

Await each full run id as described in Parent wait and delivery. Read the full
report, verify landing and content on the actual source, and preserve failed
work for an explicit recovery decision. Never hand-merge around native run
ownership. Run the project's gate again on integrated work. Deploy or push only
with repository/user authorization; a missing policy grants neither. Report
what is ready to try, validation and review gaps, retained work, and one next
action. Stop after the accepted scope; finishing a round is not a request for
another one. On an explicit finish request, save a repository-local handoff
using `stint-handoff` (read it with `taskfleet skill print stint-handoff` if
not loaded). If that text is unavailable, record accepted intent, delivered
results, evidence gaps, unresolved owned runs, and the next action in `TODO.md`,
commit exact paths, and disclose that the full handoff workflow was unavailable. The conductor edits handoff
and decision records only; product edits remain worktree workers' work.


# stint-handoff
## Without issuectl: repository-local handoff


If the optional repository tracker is not enabled, use this path instead of
the tracker-backed handoff below. Read the repository's handoff convention and
record the user's decisions in the documents that own them, not in a new tracker.
Inspect every full run id this session launched or explicitly adopted with
`taskfleet run show`; do not infer ownership from a global list. Read reports,
verify delivered content, and inventory retained work even for terminal runs.
Do not cancel, recover, or discard foreign runs. A live owned run or retained
work needs an explicit recovery/retention decision; record it honestly and do
not claim the handoff resolved it.

Write the repository's existing handoff file (default `TODO.md`) with accepted
intent, landed results and validation evidence, decisions still needed, known
unresolved owned run ids and recovery actions, and the next bounded task. Do
not manufacture issue slugs, lanes, reservations, a backlog, or a private DAG.
There is no DAG verification step in this mode. Preserve other sessions' edits;
stage only your exact paths, inspect the staged diff, and commit the narrative
separately from other changes. Push only if repository policy authorizes it.

If an external session-saving skill is available, read its actual contract;
otherwise save durable repository lessons in the most specific existing agent
instructions or owning document and commit those separately. Do not write
personal/global preferences implicitly. A test-account reset is neither assumed
nor automatic: perform one only with explicit project/user authorization and
disclose its result. Report what was saved, unresolved work and failures, and
one next action; a required failure prevents claiming completion.


# night-stint-prep
## Without issuectl: prepare accepted tasks, not a private schedule


If the optional repository tracker is not enabled, use this path instead of
the tracker-backed preparation below. Read repository instructions, the current
handoff and the user's accepted scope. Choose a bounded next task that can be
completed without human decisions; name its goal, done criteria, exact gate,
and what must wait for the user. Describe follow-on intent only as conditional
on the first task landing, not as a new graph or tracker. The next conductor
works serially from this accepted scope and Taskfleet's live reports, checking
for overlapping runs before spawning. No lane, issue slug, reservation file,
or private DAG is required or created.

Write and commit the repository's handoff file (default `TODO.md`), preserving
useful history and other sessions' edits. Distinguish implementation, evidence
preparation, and human-gated decisions. Include validation/deployment limits,
unverified claims, known unresolved run ids, and the morning report expected.
No workers are launched here. Push only under repository policy. Night work
grants no extra decision, publishing, credential, or deployment authority.
Brief the user on the chosen scope and remaining human decisions.


# worktree-spinoff
## Choosing a profile and creating the run


Profile names are installation-owned, not universal capability tiers. Use the
configured default by omitting `--profile`, unless the caller or repository
explicitly selects an available name. Describe the required capability and risk
in the brief, not a presumed local model or launcher. Preflight the complete
intended create with `--dry-run`; an unknown explicit profile or any other
preflight error stops creation, without silently substituting a profile. The
real call uses the selector whose preflight passed. For child creates, preflight
without both parent flags (child dry-run is unsupported), then restore them on
the real call with an idempotency key. On `profile_required`, surface the
configured choices and ask for a selection; do not invent or silently choose
one. Profile selection is not review evidence.

```
# First issue this command with --dry-run; remove only --dry-run after it passes.
# skill-example-ci: skip
taskfleet run create \
  --kind spinoff \
  --title "<2–4 word title>" \
  --task "<self-contained brief>" \
  [--profile <resolved-profile>] \
  [--source-branch <branch>] \
  [--headless | --tmux-session <name>] \
  [--notify <cmd>] \
  [--parent-run-id <id> --parent-node-id <id>] \
  [--idempotency-key <key>] \
  [--dry-run]
```

What the flags do that `--help` does not tell you:

- Exactly one of `--task` and `--prompt-file`; empty or whitespace-only text is
  rejected, so do not strip it silently. `--parent-run-id` and
  `--parent-node-id` come together or not at all.
- `--idempotency-key` makes the call safe to repeat after a transient error: the
  same key returns the original run, with `idempotent_replay: true` in the
  envelope, instead of spawning twice.
- `--notify <cmd>` is the push signal: the supervisor runs it via `sh -c` on the
  terminal transition, before teardown, with `TASKFLEET_RUN_ID`,
  `TASKFLEET_STATUS`, `TASKFLEET_SUMMARY`, `TASKFLEET_RUN_KIND`, and
  `TASKFLEET_RUN_TITLE` in the environment, and again for an unresolved
  `node.awaiting_input` after the grace window with
  `TASKFLEET_STATUS=awaiting-input`, `TASKFLEET_AWAITING_INPUT=1`, and the
  discussion array in `TASKFLEET_AWAITING_INPUT_JSON`. Delivery is at least
  once: a supervisor crash between firing and recording its marker re-fires on
  restart, because a missed completion is worse than a duplicate, so the command
  should tolerate running twice. This is an optional operator-supplied hook,
  not a required parent wake mechanism; use the plain wait process above.
- Output defaults to `--output jsonl`, one compact envelope per line.
